---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/faraday-cup-net-charge-surface-patterns';
const title = 'Faraday Cup Measurements: Net Charge and Surface Patterns';
const seoTitle = 'Faraday Cup Net Charge and Surface Patterns';
const description = 'Explore Faraday cup measurements through two research sources and an original arithmetic example for reviewing a material report before equipment selection.';
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-11-05', dateModified: '2026-11-05', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Faraday Cup Net Charge</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Static Control</span><span class="blog-tag">Charge Measurement</span><span class="blog-tag">Insulating Materials</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">A laboratory report assigns a positive net charge to a material sample. Does that tell you which parts of its surface are positive? Understanding what the result contains helps keep a material review focused before selecting static-control equipment.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>November 5, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>3 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/net-charge-surface-patterns-v1.jpg" alt="Two hypothetical charge patterns with the same positive signed sum." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original arithmetic illustration. Symbols represent equal model charge contributions, not measured surface regions.</em></p>

      <section id="net-charge-quantity">
        <h2>Identify the net-charge result</h2>
        <p>In their <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2411.09505v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">author manuscript on charged granular materials</a>, O’Hara and colleagues describe a basic Faraday cup as measuring the net charge of material collected in it. They also distinguish a Faraday pail arrangement that measures through induction without particle contact. Keep the arrangement and the reported quantity identified when reading a result.</p>
        <p>Their study also explores information within a time-varying powder trace using additional measurements and modelling. That is a richer analysis than one reported net value. The question here is what a single signed total tells you, rather than how to build or operate a test fixture.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="opposite-sign-regions">
        <h2>A net polarity can coexist with opposite-sign regions</h2>
        <p><a href="https://www.research.ed.ac.uk/en/publications/the-mosaic-of-surface-charge-in-contact-electrification/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Baytekin and colleagues’ 2011 study</a> reported contact-electrified dielectric samples with a net positive or negative polarity while their surfaces contained both positive and negative regions. This was an experimental finding for the materials examined. It does not establish the pattern, size or strength of charged regions on an untested production film.</p>
        <p>The practical distinction is between a signed total and a spatial distribution. A positive net result alone does not demonstrate that every region has positive charge.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="charge-sum-example">
        <h2>Read the equal-charge example</h2>
        <p>In our illustration, let <em>q</em> be one positive model charge unit. Every plus symbol contributes +<em>q</em>; every minus symbol contributes −<em>q</em>. The drawings are hypothetical and use no measured values.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pattern A:</strong> three positive contributions give 3<em>q</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Pattern B:</strong> five positive and two negative contributions give 5<em>q</em> − 2<em>q</em> = 3<em>q</em>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The totals match, but the contributions and their locations differ. Adding them loses that location information. This is arithmetic, not a reconstruction of the research images or a prediction of a Faraday instrument’s accuracy.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="material-review">
        <h2>Apply the distinction to a material review</h2>
        <p>Imagine two sample reports with the same net-charge value. A purchasing note calls the samples “electrostatically identical” and proposes using one as a substitute for the other. These are invented report summaries, not DGSDK test results.</p>
        <p>The note asks the total to establish more than it contains. Return the discussion to the actual decision: what property must the substitute demonstrate for this application? If the concern is tied to a particular part of the material, ask the testing team what evidence would address that location. Keep any untested equivalence visible in the decision.</p>
        <p>If the supplied record instead contains a noncontact field or voltage reading, use our <a href="/blog/surface-resistance-vs-surface-voltage">guide to interpreting film electrical measurements</a> to identify that quantity. It should not be relabelled as a Faraday net-charge result.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Discuss the material question behind your static problem</h2>
        <p>Share the material description, process location and available measurement report. Our team can discuss the static-control application and the information needed for equipment selection. The cited studies do not establish performance for a Shidike ionizer.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/static-eliminator">Explore Static Control Equipment &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Material%20Charge%20Measurement%20Review">Discuss your static-control application &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
